--- MetricFiltration.lean
+++ MetricFiltration.lean
@@ -302,4 +302,37 @@
     ripsGraph α ε₁ = ripsGraph α ε₂ := by
   rw [ripsGraph_bot_of_neg h₁, ripsGraph_bot_of_neg h₂]
 
+#check coveringNumber_antitone
+#check sphere_perturbation_stability
+#check maximal_packing_is_cover
+
+/-
+-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--
+
+Hypothesis. Metric filtrations should expose three complementary controls needed
+for finite-data topology: monotonic graph growth, antitone covering complexity,
+and robustness of geometric sphere constraints under perturbation.
+
+Experiment. The Rips construction is monotone at every real scale and collapses
+to the empty graph at negative scales. Covering numbers decrease as the radius
+increases, while the packing-covering theorem turns a maximal separated sample
+into a cover. The concrete real-line examples exercise the scale boundaries.
+
+Analysis. These facts share an order-theoretic core: enlarging a scale weakens a
+distance predicate. The framework extends more broadly to arbitrary ordered index
+sets and to other monotone proximity graphs.
+
+Critique. The covering number is defined through finite covers and does not itself
+recover homology. In a pseudometric boundary case, distinct zero-distance points
+may create edges at scale zero, so the zero-scale emptiness theorem correctly
+requires a metric space. Maximal packing is used through an explicit maximality
+condition rather than inferred from packing alone.
+
+Synthesis. Metric filtration monotonicity, covering antitonicity, perturbation
+stability, and packing-covering duality form the geometric foundation for the
+subsequent finite Rips-complex stability results without asserting an unsupported
+sphere-recognition principle.
+-- !--
+-/
+
 
